Notebook[{
Cell["Period-three ether erosion with a white right tail", "Title",
 CellID->874461601,ExpressionUUID->"22a1a762-10e3-861e-525e-3f4ca097ed64"],

Cell["Troy Lowry; OpenAI Codex \[Bullet] 2026-10-24", "Author",
 CellID->36768919,ExpressionUUID->"4da796ef-9f57-a4bf-7b5c-0fc91ae30e85"],

Cell["\<\
For every Rule 30 evolution of the right half whose boundary follows the \
period-three ether from time T, whiteness beyond W at T forces every prefix \
1..k to depart from the matching ether by T + 3 max(W + 12 - k, 0) + 6, for \
every k >= 1 (Rule30Submission.white_tail_erosion). Column one departs \
arbitrarily late (white_tail_often_departs). Spatial width, phase, transient \
and initial word are arbitrary. The earlier erosion theorem of Luis Perez \
Morales and Claude treats finite perturbations of the ether, which have a \
rightmost defect. A white tail instead has infinitely many ether defects. The \
new argument replaces a rightmost defect by a defect witness bounded by an \
edge of speed one (erosion_bounded). A consecutive run of the earlier proof's \
bad front state advances by at least eight per step, so it is finite; its \
unchanged good-state potential then forces erosion. Locality and one spatial \
period of the ether supply the witness. This is an extension of a partial \
result. No eventual center period is excluded, no optimal deadline is \
claimed, and arbitrary infinite right sides remain open.\
\>", "Abstract",
 CellID->1699946459,ExpressionUUID->"1b39f900-6440-cee7-4ac6-f16a0dfa94cd"],

Cell["The result and its scope", "Section",
 CellID->1535504786,ExpressionUUID->"75d93363-5da6-51a4-23e6-ab4d5453238e"],

Cell["\<\
Let F(t,p) be a Rule 30 evolution for columns p >= 1. Column zero is driven \
by the period-three ether E(j,t), at any attachment j0 and phase s, from time \
T onward. Suppose F(T,p)=0 for every p>W. For every k>=1 there is a time t \
and a column p such that\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1208482393,ExpressionUUID->"25a568a6-87a2-527f-535a-810032572145"],

Cell["\<\
    T <= t <= T + 3 max(W + 12 - k, 0) + 6,     1 <= p <= k, and F(t,p) != \
E(j0+p,t+s).\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->28909615,ExpressionUUID->"00d7f70d-8d0e-ae34-30ec-ef5724b8f51e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The prefix need not match initially: if it already differs, t=T works. \
Restarting the argument at later times shows that column one departs from its \
matching ether profile arbitrarily late. The two headline theorems are ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Rule30Submission", ".", "white_tail"}], "_erosion"}]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2f3e8453-9762-68ea-56c5-1e1d22173a30"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"white_tail", "_often", "_departs"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5902d484-617f-9570-0f31-cdee100bfc5a"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellID->193832008,ExpressionUUID->"56e0e12c-de7d-6135-7e9f-f889db08301f"],

Cell["\<\
This does not prove or disprove a Rule 30 Prize problem. In particular, \
departing from one matching ether profile does not rule out other periodic \
profiles. The eventual-period-three center question remains open.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->684317082,ExpressionUUID->"5708d032-2150-04f2-7d37-ca7c50e56ed8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ArrayPlot", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"CellularAutomaton", "[", 
    RowBox[{"30", ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}], ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"48", ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "48"}], ",", "48"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"Frame", "->", "True"}], ",", "\n", "  ", 
   RowBox[{"PlotLabel", "->", "\"\<Rule 30 from a single black cell\>\""}]}], 
  "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[1]:= ",
 CellID->24617741,ExpressionUUID->"3b7807e8-9504-b25a-6907-9eb21cb600a0"],

